<p>Just to share with you a couple my thoughts that seem pertinent to questions you raise:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I would think that anything that would keep the lid on the cannister would have to be attached to the can itself, rather than being part of a room.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>My mental picture had them under some giant hydraulic piston in some reinforced chamber somewhere. A &quot;boot&quot; for the can seems it would be insufficient for anticipated future breaches.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Then how was anyone able to get the cannisters while they were still shut? Is there some specific external event that triggers this? Or does it just spontaneously happen at random?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Just work backwards: As more cans are opened, the internal pressure of the other cans increases. So when fewer cans were open, the internal pressure was less. When no cans were open, the internal pressure was probably low enough to be held back by the lids alone.</p>

